1
00:00:01,300 --> 00:00:03,400
I stepped out of the
car to take one photo.

2
00:00:03,562 --> 00:00:04,962
My eyelashes froze.

3
00:00:05,125 --> 00:00:06,737
I didn't know I had eyelashes.

4
00:00:06,913 --> 00:00:10,237
My camera took one look at the
weather and switched itself off.

5
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:12,550
This is a fish.

6
00:00:12,725 --> 00:00:14,038
I bought it in that village.

7
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:15,575
It stands up on its own.

8
00:00:15,738 --> 00:00:21,063
The village is Oymyakon, often called the
coldest permanently inhabited place on

9
00:00:21,113 --> 00:00:21,913
Earth.

10
00:00:21,875 --> 00:00:25,950
In nineteen thirty-three, its weather
station recorded about minus sixty-eight

11
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:26,937
Celsius.

12
00:00:27,113 --> 00:00:29,175
That's about minus ninety Fahrenheit.

13
00:00:29,338 --> 00:00:33,125
In July twenty ten, the same
station hit plus thirty-four.

14
00:00:33,287 --> 00:00:35,237
Roughly five hundred people live there.

15
00:00:35,412 --> 00:00:36,212
They chose it.

16
00:00:36,350 --> 00:00:37,150
Why?

17
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:39,738
I didn't go as a tourist.

18
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:40,888
I went to move in.

19
00:00:41,062 --> 00:00:44,150
By the end of the trip, I knew
why people really live there.

20
00:00:44,312 --> 00:00:46,587
The answer is written
in the village's name,

21
00:00:46,638 --> 00:00:47,800
and it isn't the cold.

22
00:00:47,975 --> 00:00:50,012
I also knew whether I was unpacking.

23
00:00:51,213 --> 00:00:54,162
Getting there took a plane,
then a very long drive,

24
00:00:54,275 --> 00:00:55,488
then more of the drive.

25
00:00:55,650 --> 00:00:57,825
My map was upside down for most of it.

26
00:00:57,988 --> 00:00:59,538
I arrived in late December.

27
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:03,675
Around then, the sun is up for only
about four and a half hours a day.

28
00:01:03,838 --> 00:01:06,038
I arrived during one
of the hours it wasn't.

29
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:09,400
January averages around
minus forty-six Celsius.

30
00:01:09,562 --> 00:01:11,737
Call it minus fifty-one Fahrenheit.

31
00:01:11,912 --> 00:01:14,412
The sign at the edge of
the village was very small.

32
00:01:14,575 --> 00:01:15,713
So was the village.

33
00:01:15,888 --> 00:01:17,175
That's where I took the photo.

34
00:01:18,613 --> 00:01:21,350
Standing by that tiny
sign, I had a question.

35
00:01:21,513 --> 00:01:23,038
Why is there a village here at all?

36
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:25,512
Somebody picked this spot, on purpose.

37
00:01:25,675 --> 00:01:26,600
I had guesses.

38
00:01:26,762 --> 00:01:28,163
My first guess was the view.

39
00:01:28,325 --> 00:01:29,188
It was dark.

40
00:01:29,363 --> 00:01:30,812
My second guess was fishing.

41
00:01:30,975 --> 00:01:32,600
I tapped the river with a stick.

42
00:01:32,775 --> 00:01:34,088
The river tapped back.

43
00:01:34,250 --> 00:01:37,575
My third guess was that nobody
checked the weather before moving in.

44
00:01:37,738 --> 00:01:39,075
I liked my third guess.

45
00:01:39,238 --> 00:01:40,562
My third guess was wrong.

46
00:01:41,976 --> 00:01:44,151
Turns out the answer
was hiding in the name.

47
00:01:44,314 --> 00:01:48,701
Oymyakon is usually explained as
meaning water that does not freeze.

48
00:01:48,863 --> 00:01:50,801
Linguists argue about the roots.

49
00:01:50,964 --> 00:01:53,951
Some sources trace the
word to the Even language,

50
00:01:54,001 --> 00:01:56,051
while others point to Sakha.

51
00:01:56,226 --> 00:01:58,926
But the story told about
this valley is the same.

52
00:01:59,088 --> 00:02:03,239
Nearby there were warm springs, water
that stayed open through the winter.

53
00:02:03,401 --> 00:02:04,651
So I went looking for one.

54
00:02:04,814 --> 00:02:06,251
I walked past it twice.

55
00:02:06,414 --> 00:02:07,251
It was steaming.

56
00:02:07,414 --> 00:02:08,613
I was reading my map.

57
00:02:08,776 --> 00:02:10,201
My map was upside down.

58
00:02:11,629 --> 00:02:14,853
Picture a single gas
station on an empty highway,

59
00:02:14,966 --> 00:02:16,691
at night, in a blizzard.

60
00:02:16,854 --> 00:02:18,566
You don't stop there because it's pretty.

61
00:02:18,729 --> 00:02:23,103
Reindeer herders stopped at the springs to
water their animals and rest before long

62
00:02:23,153 --> 00:02:23,966
journeys.

63
00:02:24,129 --> 00:02:26,804
If you move reindeer
across a frozen landscape,

64
00:02:26,928 --> 00:02:29,191
open water isn't a curiosity.

65
00:02:29,353 --> 00:02:30,354
It's the whole point.

66
00:02:30,516 --> 00:02:32,778
A stopping place became a regular camp.

67
00:02:32,941 --> 00:02:36,328
Over time, the camp grew
into a permanent village.

68
00:02:36,491 --> 00:02:38,853
So the village didn't
appear despite the cold.

69
00:02:39,016 --> 00:02:42,204
It appeared because, in the
middle of all that cold,

70
00:02:42,316 --> 00:02:44,128
something refused to freeze.

71
00:02:44,291 --> 00:02:46,328
I dipped my water bottle into the spring.

72
00:02:46,529 --> 00:02:47,954
For once, it sloshed.

73
00:02:49,968 --> 00:02:52,830
Next, I went to find the
chilliest spot in town.

74
00:02:53,006 --> 00:02:54,680
That was the whole point of the move.

75
00:02:54,843 --> 00:02:58,130
Oymyakon has a monument
for it, the Pole of Cold,

76
00:02:58,268 --> 00:03:00,943
so I stood next to it
and tried to look famous.

77
00:03:01,118 --> 00:03:02,118
Why a monument?

78
00:03:02,280 --> 00:03:06,618
Because the record that made Oymyakon famous
came from the local weather station in

79
00:03:06,668 --> 00:03:07,818
nineteen thirty-three.

80
00:03:07,981 --> 00:03:12,806
On the sixth of February, it recorded
about minus sixty-eight degrees Celsius.

81
00:03:12,981 --> 00:03:15,043
That's about minus ninety Fahrenheit.

82
00:03:15,205 --> 00:03:19,330
Guinness World Records lists it
as minus sixty-seven point seven.

83
00:03:19,505 --> 00:03:24,330
The World Meteorological Organization
lists minus sixty-seven point eight.

84
00:03:24,493 --> 00:03:26,631
So even the number comes with an argument.

85
00:03:26,793 --> 00:03:30,693
Either way, it's the lowest temperature on
record for a place where people live all

86
00:03:30,743 --> 00:03:31,543
year.

87
00:03:31,506 --> 00:03:32,306
Well.

88
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:32,980
Almost.

89
00:03:33,068 --> 00:03:37,930
Far to the northwest, the town of
Verkhoyansk has its own reading from eighteen

90
00:03:37,980 --> 00:03:40,443
ninety-two, of about the same number.

91
00:03:40,618 --> 00:03:43,168
Some sources say the two
towns share the record.

92
00:03:43,330 --> 00:03:46,955
Others treat the Verkhoyansk
reading as disputed.

93
00:03:47,118 --> 00:03:49,693
I measured the distance
on my map with my fingers.

94
00:03:49,855 --> 00:03:52,918
I decided Verkhoyansk could keep its half.

95
00:03:54,336 --> 00:03:55,636
Then it got complicated.

96
00:03:55,811 --> 00:04:00,161
The weather station behind that famous
number isn't in Oymyakon itself.

97
00:04:00,324 --> 00:04:05,111
It sits in a valley between Oymyakon and
a neighbouring village called Tomtor,

98
00:04:05,174 --> 00:04:06,649
near Tomtor's airfield.

99
00:04:06,824 --> 00:04:10,186
The station and the airfield
both date from the Soviet era.

100
00:04:10,361 --> 00:04:14,086
Some sources argue that
Tomtor, not Oymyakon,

101
00:04:14,236 --> 00:04:15,924
is the real Pole of Cold.

102
00:04:16,086 --> 00:04:19,586
Oymyakon, meanwhile, has
the official monument.

103
00:04:19,761 --> 00:04:23,974
So a village got famous partly because
someone finally put a thermometer nearby.

104
00:04:24,161 --> 00:04:27,098
Thermometers are very good
at making places famous.

105
00:04:27,261 --> 00:04:28,373
I went to the station.

106
00:04:28,548 --> 00:04:29,949
Then back to the monument.

107
00:04:30,124 --> 00:04:33,786
Then I tried standing exactly
halfway, so I could live in both.

108
00:04:33,948 --> 00:04:36,386
Halfway turned out to
be mostly frozen road.

109
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:40,975
Lying on that road, I finally
asked a better question.

110
00:04:41,150 --> 00:04:42,675
Why is it this cold here at all?

111
00:04:42,838 --> 00:04:45,762
Oymyakon isn't even
inside the Arctic Circle.

112
00:04:45,925 --> 00:04:48,512
It sits a few degrees
of latitude south of it.

113
00:04:48,675 --> 00:04:50,987
The reason is boring and brilliant.

114
00:04:51,150 --> 00:04:56,338
In winter, a huge, stable zone of high
pressure called the Siberian High settles

115
00:04:56,388 --> 00:04:57,738
over this part of Asia.

116
00:04:57,900 --> 00:05:03,112
It brings clear, dry skies, and clear skies
let the ground lose its heat straight up

117
00:05:03,162 --> 00:05:04,200
into space.

118
00:05:04,375 --> 00:05:05,437
Then there's the valley.

119
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:06,675
Cold air is heavy.

120
00:05:06,850 --> 00:05:09,900
It slides downhill and
pools on the valley floor,

121
00:05:09,950 --> 00:05:13,938
like water in a bowl, while the
mountains around it keep warmer air out.

122
00:05:14,100 --> 00:05:18,325
Researchers measuring this valley have found
the mountain slopes above it can be far

123
00:05:18,375 --> 00:05:20,712
warmer in winter than the valley floor.

124
00:05:20,875 --> 00:05:22,550
So the cold isn't a spot.

125
00:05:22,713 --> 00:05:23,675
It's the whole bowl.

126
00:05:23,850 --> 00:05:25,162
I thought about climbing out.

127
00:05:25,325 --> 00:05:26,362
Up there it's warmer.

128
00:05:26,525 --> 00:05:27,888
Up there it's also a mountain.

129
00:05:28,062 --> 00:05:29,087
I stayed in the bowl.

130
00:05:30,804 --> 00:05:32,816
I'm going to put the
fish down for this part.

131
00:05:32,992 --> 00:05:35,991
The same era brought something
much darker to this region.

132
00:05:36,154 --> 00:05:40,629
In the nineteen thirties, the Soviet Union
built a network of forced labour camps

133
00:05:40,679 --> 00:05:44,304
across the Kolyma region,
part of the Gulag system,

134
00:05:44,417 --> 00:05:45,892
largely to mine gold.

135
00:05:46,067 --> 00:05:50,616
Prisoners began building the Kolyma
Highway in the early nineteen thirties,

136
00:05:50,666 --> 00:05:53,779
and forced labour continued on
it into the nineteen fifties.

137
00:05:53,942 --> 00:05:55,591
Many of them did not survive.

138
00:05:55,766 --> 00:05:58,254
The village's story
begins with the springs.

139
00:05:58,429 --> 00:06:02,579
But the highway that crosses this region
was built by people who had no choice.

140
00:06:04,809 --> 00:06:08,283
Back in the village, I tried to
get through one ordinary day.

141
00:06:08,446 --> 00:06:09,246
Morning.

142
00:06:09,171 --> 00:06:10,033
Still dark.

143
00:06:10,196 --> 00:06:14,659
Outside, a car sat in the street with
its engine running and nobody in it.

144
00:06:14,821 --> 00:06:19,371
Cars are often left running for hours,
or warmed with a fire before starting,

145
00:06:19,421 --> 00:06:22,533
because a frozen engine
may refuse to start at all.

146
00:06:22,734 --> 00:06:24,746
Think of your car on a winter morning.

147
00:06:24,908 --> 00:06:27,708
Now imagine the winter
never gives back the keys.

148
00:06:27,871 --> 00:06:30,421
I patted the car on
the hood, like a horse.

149
00:06:30,596 --> 00:06:31,771
The car kept running.

150
00:06:31,921 --> 00:06:33,096
It had places to be.

151
00:06:34,508 --> 00:06:36,271
Midday, I went to the market.

152
00:06:36,495 --> 00:06:39,533
Food here comes mostly
from animals and the river:

153
00:06:39,720 --> 00:06:44,496
meat, and fish that in winter is sold
frozen solid and stacked like firewood.

154
00:06:44,733 --> 00:06:47,596
Farming in frozen ground
is close to impossible.

155
00:06:47,771 --> 00:06:48,846
I asked for a bag.

156
00:06:49,021 --> 00:06:50,508
The fish did not need a bag.

157
00:06:50,683 --> 00:06:52,433
The fish could stand up on its own.

158
00:06:52,608 --> 00:06:53,608
That's when it clicked.

159
00:06:53,771 --> 00:06:56,670
In winter, the village
itself is the freezer.

160
00:06:56,846 --> 00:06:58,858
I carried my fish home on my shoulder,

161
00:06:58,995 --> 00:06:59,870
like a plank.

162
00:07:00,045 --> 00:07:00,845
Yes.

163
00:07:00,846 --> 00:07:01,708
This fish.

164
00:07:03,140 --> 00:07:03,940
Afternoon.

165
00:07:04,078 --> 00:07:05,140
I needed a bathroom.

166
00:07:05,315 --> 00:07:07,990
Beneath the surface the
ground is permafrost,

167
00:07:08,040 --> 00:07:12,878
frozen all year, which makes ordinary
water pipes very hard to keep working.

168
00:07:13,040 --> 00:07:14,940
Many homes use outdoor toilets.

169
00:07:15,102 --> 00:07:17,765
I looked at the path to the
outhouse for a long time.

170
00:07:17,940 --> 00:07:18,940
I made a decision.

171
00:07:19,115 --> 00:07:19,915
I waited.

172
00:07:19,965 --> 00:07:22,040
On the way back, I passed the school.

173
00:07:22,203 --> 00:07:26,390
According to Russian news reports, younger
pupils in the district get the day off

174
00:07:26,440 --> 00:07:28,627
when it reaches around minus fifty-two.

175
00:07:28,790 --> 00:07:32,215
The exact cut-off for each age
group depends on who you ask.

176
00:07:32,390 --> 00:07:35,715
Plenty of schools elsewhere close
for a light dusting of snow.

177
00:07:35,877 --> 00:07:38,140
I asked whether Oymyakon has snow days.

178
00:07:38,303 --> 00:07:39,103
It does.

179
00:07:39,228 --> 00:07:40,877
They're just extremely specific.

180
00:07:44,180 --> 00:07:44,980
Evening.

181
00:07:44,905 --> 00:07:46,218
I made tea to warm up.

182
00:07:46,392 --> 00:07:47,755
The tea did not warm up.

183
00:07:47,917 --> 00:07:50,880
It grew a thin, shiny skin and gave up.

184
00:07:51,043 --> 00:07:54,455
I sat by the window with my
scarf, my upside-down map,

185
00:07:54,518 --> 00:07:57,318
and a fish that was clearly
handling this better than me.

186
00:07:57,492 --> 00:07:59,293
The fish had made peace with everything.

187
00:07:59,455 --> 00:08:00,255
I had not.

188
00:08:00,430 --> 00:08:02,105
That night, I started packing.

189
00:08:04,124 --> 00:08:06,012
And yet, I went back in July.

190
00:08:06,187 --> 00:08:07,599
Partly out of stubbornness.

191
00:08:07,761 --> 00:08:10,849
Mostly to see whether the
village had a second personality.

192
00:08:11,011 --> 00:08:11,811
It does.

193
00:08:11,936 --> 00:08:13,574
I'd just missed the long days.

194
00:08:13,749 --> 00:08:17,162
By late June, daylight lasts
more than twenty hours.

195
00:08:17,324 --> 00:08:22,362
In July twenty ten, the station recorded
thirty-four point six degrees Celsius.

196
00:08:22,536 --> 00:08:24,449
That's about ninety-four Fahrenheit.

197
00:08:24,624 --> 00:08:27,649
Put the record low and the
record high side by side,

198
00:08:27,774 --> 00:08:30,637
and the gap is roughly one
hundred and two degrees.

199
00:08:30,799 --> 00:08:34,999
Very few places on Earth have record
highs and lows that far apart.

200
00:08:35,161 --> 00:08:36,611
I had packed the same bag.

201
00:08:36,774 --> 00:08:38,174
Everything in it was fur.

202
00:08:38,336 --> 00:08:41,699
And for one sweaty afternoon,
I wanted to move in again.

203
00:08:43,124 --> 00:08:45,349
Then I met the residents
who never go inside.

204
00:08:45,511 --> 00:08:49,424
Yakutian horses live outdoors
all year in this region,

205
00:08:49,561 --> 00:08:50,674
right through the winter.

206
00:08:50,836 --> 00:08:54,786
A genetic study published in twenty fifteen
found that they descend from domestic

207
00:08:54,836 --> 00:08:57,724
horses brought here only
a few centuries ago.

208
00:08:57,887 --> 00:09:00,786
They adapted to the cold remarkably fast,

209
00:09:00,836 --> 00:09:04,149
with compact bodies and
very thick winter coats.

210
00:09:04,312 --> 00:09:08,849
Imagine buying the right jacket, except
your body grows the jacket for you.

211
00:09:09,012 --> 00:09:11,699
My only adaptation is a large backpack.

212
00:09:11,861 --> 00:09:13,362
I tried to make friends with one.

213
00:09:13,536 --> 00:09:14,724
The horse kept eating.

214
00:09:14,899 --> 00:09:16,824
The horse has seen tourists before.

215
00:09:18,249 --> 00:09:19,624
I had also missed the party.

216
00:09:19,786 --> 00:09:23,836
Every spring, the district hosts a
festival called the Pole of Cold,

217
00:09:23,899 --> 00:09:27,974
with events in villages
including Oymyakon and Tomtor.

218
00:09:28,136 --> 00:09:32,124
Its star is Chyskhaan,
the Sakha Lord of Cold,

219
00:09:32,249 --> 00:09:34,648
who reportedly has his own ice residence.

220
00:09:34,811 --> 00:09:37,773
In twenty twenty-five, there
was even a marathon here.

221
00:09:37,936 --> 00:09:38,774
In the cold.

222
00:09:38,936 --> 00:09:39,824
On purpose.

223
00:09:39,986 --> 00:09:43,523
So the water brought the herders,
the thermometer brought the fame,

224
00:09:43,661 --> 00:09:46,224
and now the cold itself brings visitors.

225
00:09:46,374 --> 00:09:50,636
A village that grew up around water that
wouldn't freeze now welcomes people because

226
00:09:50,686 --> 00:09:52,236
of everything that does.

227
00:09:54,273 --> 00:09:56,085
So here's what my desk knows now.

228
00:09:56,260 --> 00:09:58,073
The cold was never the reason to come.

229
00:09:58,235 --> 00:09:59,610
It was the price of staying.

230
00:09:59,772 --> 00:10:00,672
Would I move there?

231
00:10:00,848 --> 00:10:02,060
The springs are lovely.

232
00:10:02,235 --> 00:10:03,447
The summer surprised me.

233
00:10:03,623 --> 00:10:06,897
But the winters are long,
dark and brutally cold,

234
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:09,047
and I lasted about one cup of tea.

235
00:10:09,210 --> 00:10:10,422
My verdict is NOPE.

236
00:10:10,585 --> 00:10:13,010
That valley is for people
who grew up with it,

237
00:10:13,185 --> 00:10:17,023
or who truly love winter:
silence, clear skies,

238
00:10:17,160 --> 00:10:18,797
and a summer that feels earned.

239
00:10:18,972 --> 00:10:20,710
The fish lives in my freezer now.

240
00:10:20,872 --> 00:10:23,048
It's the warmest winter it has ever had.

241
00:10:23,210 --> 00:10:25,247
One January there, for one July.

242
00:10:25,422 --> 00:10:26,535
Would you take that trade?
